LETTER TO ADULT PROTECTIVE SERVICES

Dear Adult Protective Services,

Thank you for taking the time to review my concerns.

I am a 62-year-old disabled resident of a licensed room-and-board home in San Bernardino County. I am writing because I have experienced an ongoing pattern of interactions with Vince, the manager of the residence, that I believe has had a significant effect on my emotional well-being.

I want to emphasize that I do not want to move. My goal is to remain in my current residence if the situation can be improved. Because I have Asperger’s syndrome and bipolar disorder, major transitions are particularly difficult for me. I have been working to establish routines, friendships, and community connections, and I would like the opportunity to continue living here in a respectful and stable environment.

Over the past several months, I have experienced repeated confrontations that I found intimidating, humiliating, or unnecessarily confrontational. These have included public criticism, questioning about my private medical information, inconsistent enforcement of house rules, and conversations in which I felt I was being singled out. I have attempted to remain calm, comply with instructions, and resolve concerns respectfully.

The continuing stress has affected my well-being. Although I cannot state that it directly caused my medical problems, I believe it contributed to increased stress, reduced sleep, and worsening symptoms related to my bipolar disorder.

I am asking Adult Protective Services to help improve the living situation rather than relocate me. My hope is that reasonable communication, respect for personal boundaries, and consistent treatment of all residents can resolve these problems while allowing me to remain in my current home.

Goals and Suggested Improvements

During my meeting with APS caseworker Jesus Garcia, I was not able to fully explain the outcomes I hope to achieve. My goal is not to create conflict but to improve the living environment for myself and the other residents. If these concerns can be addressed constructively, I would like to remain at Hernandez Room and Board.

To that end, I respectfully offer the following suggestions:

First, I recommend that the staff maintain a written rule book. This would include both the permanent house rules and any new rules adopted over time. If a rule is modified or discontinued, the original rule could be crossed out and the replacement rule entered beneath it rather than removing the old rule entirely. This would provide both staff and residents with a clear and consistent record of the current rules and reduce misunderstandings.

I previously suggested this idea to Charles and was told that it would never be adopted and that, if it were, he would fight me on it.

Second, I believe the relationship between staff and residents should be conducted as a professional relationship. Many people are comfortable being addressed by their first name in a professional setting. That is a matter of personal preference. In my own case, I prefer to be addressed as “Mr. Miller” when interacting with staff. I believe this form of address reflects the professional nature of the staff-resident relationship and communicates mutual respect. I would extend the same courtesy to staff by addressing them respectfully.

Third, I believe that routine instructions should be communicated professionally and adult-to-adult. Most interactions between staff and residents involve simple requests or reminders. In my opinion, these requests should ordinarily begin with “please” and end with “thank you.” I believe this style of communication preserves the dignity of residents while conveying exactly the same information.

Fourth, when a correction or reminder is necessary, I believe it should ordinarily be given privately rather than publicly whenever practical. In my experience, private communication is more respectful and more effective than public criticism or humiliation.

Fifth, I believe that policies regarding meals, beverages, and other routine services should be stable, clearly communicated, and applied consistently to all residents. Consistent and professionally communicated policies reduce confusion and unnecessary conflict.

Sixth, I am exploring the possibility of creating a voluntary email discussion mailing list for Hernandez Room and Board using GNU Mailman. The purpose of the mailing list would be to give residents and staff who choose to participate a convenient way to communicate between house meetings, share suggestions, announce activities, and discuss matters affecting the residence.

I am still working out the practical details, including how the mailing list would be hosted and maintained. If it proves feasible, participation would be entirely voluntary, and the mailing list would serve as an additional communication tool alongside face-to-face meetings and the shared notebook in the living room.

These suggestions are offered in a constructive spirit. My hope is to improve communication, professionalism, consistency, and the quality of life for everyone living and working at the residence.

